# Conifers (Pinidae): one leader that lays down a whorl of branches each
# year, with a few smaller branches between whorls. Branch shoots carry
# sprays of foliage and fork into flat side shoots. An apex dies in deep
# shade or once it grows too slowly to keep up; a branch with no living
# apex dies and falls a few years later. Branches sag under their own
# weight and their foliage's while their shoots turn toward the sky. In a closed stand that lifts the crown
# and leaves a clear stem; in the open the crown reaches the ground.
#
# The newest leader shoot may nod over and straighten as it stiffens, as
# in cedars and hemlocks; side shoots may branch again; and the foliage
# sprays may hang below their shoots.
#
# From adult_age, a branch apex in good light bears a cone (a seed cone,
# or a juniper's berry-like cone) with the chance cone_chance a year,
# cone_length long and pitched cone_pitch degrees down from its shoot
# (hanging, as in spruces; negative stands it up, as in firs), the shoot
# first rolled level. A cone
# stays cone_years.
#
# Revision 3 (spec rework P6): the first year the plant is adult, from
# which it bears cones, is adult_age, the name the programs that measure it
# by the plant's age share, which the rule at the seed plants sets from
# a taxon's juvenile_period_years. It replaces cone_age, with the same
# default.
#
# Revision 4 (growth plan G2.3a): the conifers' own program, renamed from
# `conifer` and homed at Pinidae, whose families extend it where they grow
# otherwise. It grows every plant as before.
#
# Revision 5 (conifer form): the lower crown of an open-grown conifer
# stays full. A branch's yearly shoot shortens with the wood between its
# apex and the stem, by 1 / (1 + (wood / branch_reach)²), not with the
# branch's age, so a well-lit limb lives as long as its light pays for
# it. Foliage lasts needle_years on average and needle_low times as long
# at the crown's foot as at its top, as leaves live longer deeper in the
# crown: 6.8 years in a western redcedar's upper third, 10.6 in its
# lower (Harlow, Duursma & Marshall 2005). Each branch shoot keeps a
# dormant bud that, after epi_delay years, sprouts a side shoot with the
# chance epi_chance a year where light reaches it (epi_light), as
# epicormic shoots refoliate old Douglas-fir limbs (Ishii & Ford 2001).
# A bud waits up to epi_years, about as long as its branch lives, at the
# foot of its shoot, so a branch whose apexes have died still dies
# though its buds wait. Side shoots grow for side_years 20, not 10, so a
# branch keeps foliage along its length, not only at its tip. A branch
# that sags to within ground_gap of the ground, or of the substrate it
# grows on (substrate@1), is shed from there out, so no limb passes into
# the ground. A year's sprays are drawn with their own turns
# (`spray(size, roll, pitch, level)`), a third of the modules they took,
# so the fuller crown fits the module limit.
#
# Revision 6 (dioecy): in a dioecious species (dioecious 1, from the
# sexual system its taxa state) a plant whose own draw `sex` is below a
# half is male and bears no seed cones (its pollen cones are too small to
# draw); the rest are female and bear them as before. A plant that bears
# both sexes (dioecious 0) grows as before.
#
# Revision 7 (conifer sag): branches sag under load (bend@1), not by
# age. A limb bends by its weight and its foliage's over its stiffness
# (green wood's density and modulus, from the Wood Handbook values a
# species states), keeps bend_set of each year's sag and straightens
# straighten of it a year, and each year's ring turns it back toward the
# line it grew in by up to reaction · length · ring width / radius²
# (reaction wood). So thin, fast-growing wood holds its line and old,
# slow limbs sag, and a crown keeps its form from 60 to 200 years where
# sag by age drew old crowns as columns of hanging limbs. The ground
# holds up a limb that sags onto it (bend@1's ground), until it is shed
# within ground_gap of it. Shoots turn
# toward the sky half as strongly (upturn), since the wood no longer
# sags by age against it.
#
# Revision 8 (conifer lives): foliage and cones live as long as their
# parameters say, no longer one year more. What a step makes is born at
# the step's end, so a rule that ends a part by its age counts the step it
# is in (`age + dt >=`), as in the other programs (generator revision
# 44).
#
# Revision 9 (full crowns): a side shoot lives as long as its light pays
# for it, as a branch has since revision 5, not side_years 20. A side
# shoot that died at 20 years took its foliage with it, so old limbs
# carried foliage only at their ends, and the crown showed the sky
# through a cage of bare limbs: at 60 years a third to two fifths of a
# spruce's, fir's, hemlock's or cedar's open-grown outline, and a
# 200-year Douglas-fir carried less foliage than a 60-year one. Its own shade and its shoots' shortening with the wood
# from the stem still end it. side_years 0 sets no calendar limit; a
# species whose branchlets fall sooner (bald cypress) still states one.
#
# Revision 10 (woody forms: architecture models): a branch apex may lay
# down a whorl of its own each year, as the trunk does, where the
# branches of Rauh's model (pines) are built like the trunk: apex_whorl
# side shoots spread round it at side_angle, turning up toward the sky,
# with the chance side_chance each, in place of the two flat side shoots
# (L and R) of Massart's model (firs, spruces), which every conifer grows
# unless its lineage sets apex_whorl. The rules at the seed plants set it
# from a taxon's architectural_model.
#
# A self-pruning conifer (crown_ratio above 0) keeps living branches only
# on the top crown_ratio of its height: a branch whose foot falls below
# that, as the tree grows, dies and falls dead_years later, so the tree
# stands on a clear bole under a high crown, as ponderosa pine does. The
# rule at the seed plants sets it from a taxon's self_pruning.
#
# Revision 11 (old pine limbs): the pipe model's exponent is a parameter,
# pipe_exponent (2.4, as before). A parent's radius to that power is the
# sum of its children's, so a higher exponent keeps a trunk thinner for
# the twigs it carries, and a species with stout shoots and heavy
# foliage tufts (pipe_tip, pipe_organ) can carry them on stout limbs
# without a trunk too thick for its age. Thin limbs under heavy tufts sag
# into whips; ponderosa pine's shoots are 0.7 to 2 cm thick (Farjon 2017).
# A spray's pipe carries its leaf area, as sapwood does a tree's foliage:
# pipe_organ is the pipe radius per metre of a spray bearing 0.25 m² per
# metre squared, the area the conifers' cards drew, and a spray that bears
# less or more takes a pipe that much smaller or larger in its flow. A
# needle card now shades with its needles' own area (generator revision
# 85), a quarter to a half of what it drew, so a crown carries two to four
# times the sprays, and without this its trunk thickened with them.
#
# Short shoots (revision 11 too): a branch shoot of a conifer with short
# shoots (spur_years above 0, as in larches) leafs out again from them
# each year for spur_years after its first, spur_share as fully as in its
# first year, so a deciduous larch (needle_years under a year) carries its
# needle tufts along the older wood of its branches, not only on the
# year's new shoots: Farjon (2017) gives Larix a strong long- and short-
# shoot dimorphism, the needles mostly in tufts of 15 to 45 on short
# shoots. With spur_years 0 a shoot leafs out once, as before.
#
# Slow shoots (revision 11 too): an apex dies once its shoot would be
# shorter than min_shoot, now 5 mm where it was 25 mm. A limb's shoots
# shorten with its reach, so a narrow-crowned conifer (a small
# branch_reach) lost every side shoot past its first metre to that cut,
# and its limbs went bare to a tuft at the tip: subalpine fir, whose
# crown FEIS gives as "extremely narrow and dense ... with short
# branches", drew poles. Shaded and slow conifer shoots grow a few
# millimetres to a few centimetres a year (general knowledge, not
# measured); light still decides which apexes live (shade_death). Fast
# crowns, whose shoots stay longer than 25 mm, grow as before.
#
# Revision 12 (nodding leaders): a leader that nods (nod above 0, as in
# hemlocks, whose drooping leader is the genus's field mark) carries its
# year's foliage in two sprays, one where each half of the shoot starts,
# so the needles follow the bend. The one spray it had stood at the
# shoot's foot and ran on straight past the bend, which left the nodding
# tip a bare stick above the crown. The two together have the one's
# area. A leader that does not nod grows as before.
#
# Several stems (revision 12 too): a clump of stems leaders grows from one
# foot, the second and later ones leaning stem_angle out, as whitebark pine
# grows 2 to 5 stems together from a nutcracker's seed cache (Farjon 2017;
# FEIS: 6 to 47% of trees single-stemmed, by site). One stem grows as
# before.
#
# Revision 13 (old trees): grown for centuries, conifers kept every limb
# whose apex lived, so wood piled up: at 100 years an open-grown
# Douglas-fir carried 1.4 million segments, doubling every 20 years, and
# Douglas-fir, western hemlock and Sitka spruce passed the 4 million
# module limit before 150 years. A limb whose tips catch too little light
# for the segments it carries is now shed (limb_light, scaled by
# shade_death); all three grow to 150 years, hemlock and spruce to 300.
# And a shoot sets side buds by its length: one shorter than bud_length
# sets proportionally fewer, and a leader shoot that short fewer whorl
# branches. Every shoot had set a full set however short it was, so a
# slow tree (whitebark pine's leader grows about 2 cm a year as a seedling,
# FEIS) packed thousands of shoots into a sapling's crown and smothered
# itself.
# And a branch's yearly shoot may be a share of the leader's that year
# (apical; Wilson 2000's apical control) instead of a fixed length slowed
# with the branch's reach. A whorl's branches then lengthen as the leader
# above them rises, so the crown is a cone whose breadth is that share:
# with a fixed length the branches near the top grew as fast as the
# leader allowed room for, and the crowns stood as tubes, their top
# halves twice as wide as photographs of open-grown trees show
# (conifers/crown-profiles-photos-2026-10-17.md in the PlantGen
# workspace). Once the leader slows near the tree's height the branches
# keep at least branch_floor, and the reach slows them too, so an old
# limb stops lengthening. Off by default.
# Old limbs also branch again: with side_order 2 the side shoots set twigs,
# which live twig_years and carry their foliage in one spray of the three
# sprays' area (a third of the modules), and spent buds more than 2 m above
# ground_watch go. Branching once, an old crown was a fishbone of bare
# whips; with all three a fully branched Sitka spruce grows to 300 years
# within the limit. Branch shoots lengthen over top_ramp metres below the
# treetop (Cannell et al. 1976), so young tops are pointed, not blunt. And
# bole clears a tree's foot of limbs in step with its height: saplings keep
# limbs to the ground, old trees show the clean trunk photographs and
# Strieby's (2017) Sitka spruce plate show.
# Grown on past 150 years, those crowns still lost half to nine tenths of
# their foliage by 300 (Douglas-fir 1819 to 195 m², western larch 105 to
# 7): not to shade, as their crowns grew brighter, but because they stopped
# renewing their twigs. Every order's shoot was a share of the leader's
# rise, so once the leader stopped, side shoots grew a centimetre a year
# and, shorter than bud_length, set a tenth of their twigs. With apical, a
# side shoot (order 2 and up) now grows at least side_floor before
# side_growth,
# coming in over floor_ramp metres below the treetop so the top stays
# narrow; dormant buds may be kept only up to epi_order (on a limb's
# main axis, where old Douglas-fir's epicormic shoots stand, Ishii & Ford
# 2001), so they can wait the limb's life within the module limit; and a
# limb whose tips' mean light falls under limb_tip times shade_death is
# shed, so a lineage can ease limb_light, which sheds a limb for the
# segments it carries and so, in the end, for its age. And with seg_min an
# apex holds its growth until it reaches seg_min and lays it down as one
# shoot, its sprays as large in area as the years' it holds (below the
# top_ramp metres of the treetop, whose short shoots lay down every year so
# a young top is not a bare stick), so an old
# limb's slow shoots cost a segment, a bud and a spray every few years, not
# every year: a fully branched old Douglas-fir or western hemlock then fits
# the module and segment limits without shedding its inner side shoots.
# All four are off by default.
# Limb shedding (limb_light) and short shoots' fewer buds (bud_length) are
# off by default, set by the families fitted with them, so a yew or a
# cypress grows as before. And shade strengthens apical control
# (apical_light): a branch in less light than apical_light, scaled by
# min(1, shade_death / 0.1)^6, takes a proportionally smaller share of the
# leader's shoot, so a shade-intolerant tree grown in a stand keeps a
# narrow crown while its leader races up, where a shade-tolerant fir or
# hemlock keeps spreading. Taking the full share in any light, a
# stand-grown Douglas-fir spread its shaded limbs 4 m at 30 years and
# carried four times the needles, and its trunk grew twice as thick.
#
# One step is one year. Angles are degrees; lengths are metres.

lsystem pinidae 13;

# Height of the leader over time: a Chapman-Richards curve,
# height = height_max * (1 - exp(-height_rate * age))^height_shape.
param height_max = 70;
param height_rate = 0.018;
param height_shape = 1.5;
param leader_shade = 0.35;     # fraction of the leader's growth kept in full shade
param nod = 0;                 # degrees the newest leader shoot nods over
param nod_years = 2;           # years a leader shoot takes to stand up straight

param whorl = 5;               # branches per annual whorl
param interwhorl = 2;          # smaller branches along each year's leader shoot
param interwhorl_vigour = 0.5; # their growth relative to whorl branches
param limb_age = 20;           # years before a limb may be shed for want of light (revision 13)
param limb_light = 0;          # a limb older than limb_age is shed when its tips' light, qsum, falls under this per segment it carries, times shade_death / 0.1; 0: never
param limb_tip = 0;            # a limb older than limb_age is shed when its tips' mean light falls under this times shade_death; 0: never
param bud_length = 0;          # a shoot shorter than this sets proportionally fewer side buds, a leader fewer whorl branches; 0: all
param stems = 1;               # stems from one foot, as a clump grows from a bird's seed cache
param stem_angle = 15;         # degrees each stem of a clump leans out
param branch_angle = 64;       # angle between the leader and a new branch
param upturn = 0.0075;         # how strongly shoots bend toward the sky
param branch_growth = 0.3;     # yearly shoot of a first-order branch in full light
param branch_reach = 6;        # wood from the stem at which a branch's yearly shoot is half as long
param reach_power = 2;         # the power of wood / branch_reach in that slowing: higher keeps a young limb's growth and stops an old limb nearer branch_reach
param apical = 0;              # a first-order branch's yearly shoot in full light is this share of the leader's that year (apical control); 0: branch_growth, slowed by branch_reach
param branch_floor = 0.02;     # with apical, the least yearly shoot of a first-order branch in full light, once the leader slows
param side_floor = 0;          # with apical, the least yearly shoot of a side shoot (order 2 and up) in full light, before side_growth, once the leader slows; 0: branch_floor
param seg_min = 0;             # an apex holds its growth until it reaches this many metres and lays it down as one shoot, its sprays as large in area as the years' it holds, so slow old shoots cost a segment every few years; 0: every year
param floor_ramp = 0;          # side_floor comes in over this many metres below the treetop, so the top stays pointed; 0: in full at once
param apical_light = 0;        # with apical, a branch whose light is under this, times min(1, shade_death / 0.1)^6, takes a proportionally smaller share of the leader's shoot (shade strengthens apical control, less in a shade-tolerant species); 0: off
param top_ramp = 0;            # depth below the treetop, m, over which branch shoots lengthen to full (Cannell et al. 1976: lateral shoots are shortest in the top whorls), so the top is pointed; 0: off
param side_growth = 0.55;      # side shoots grow this fraction as fast
param side_chance = 0.75;      # chance of a side shoot on each side each year
param side_angle = 50;         # angle between a branch and its side shoots
param side_order = 1;          # highest branch order that forms side shoots
param twig_years = 0;          # years a shoot of the highest order (one that sets no side shoots) keeps growing; 0 while its light pays
param side_years = 0;          # years a side shoot keeps growing; 0 while its light pays
param crown_ratio = 0;         # share of its height, from the top, on which a self-pruning tree keeps living branches; 0: all
param bole = 0;                # share of its height that a tree near height_max has cleared of limbs, cleared in step with height: limbs below bole * (height / height_max)^bole_shape * height die, so saplings keep branches to the ground and old trees show a clean trunk; 0: off
param bole_shape = 3;
param apex_whorl = 0;          # side shoots a branch apex spreads round itself each year (Rauh's model); 0: two flat ones
param min_shoot = 0.005;       # an apex dies once its shoot would be shorter (revision 11; 0.025 before)
param shade_death = 0.1;       # light below which an apex starves
param shade_years = 3;         # years in a row an apex survives starving
param ground_gap = 0.1;        # a branch is shed where it comes this close to the ground
param ground_watch = 1;        # height below which a side shoot's foot watches the ground
param dead_years = 4;          # years a dead branch stays on the tree
param needle_years = 7;        # years a shoot keeps its foliage, on average through the crown
param needle_low = 1.56;       # how many times as long at the crown's foot as at its top
param epi_delay = 5;           # years a branch's dormant bud waits before it may sprout
param epi_light = 0.3;         # light a dormant bud needs to sprout
param epi_chance = 0.03;       # chance a year that a waiting bud in the light sprouts
param epi_years = 300;         # years a dormant bud lives
param epi_order = 0;           # highest branch order whose shoots keep a dormant bud; 0: side_order
param spray_base = 0.2;        # foliage spray length on the shortest shoot
param spray_length = 1.3;      # and how much longer per metre of shoot
param twig_angle = 42;         # angle of the twig sprays beside each shoot
param spray_droop = 0;         # degrees the twig sprays hang below their shoot
param spray_area = 0.3;        # leaf area of a spray 1 m long, m²
param pipe_exponent = 2.4;     # a parent's radius to this power is its children's summed (revision 11)
param spur_years = 0;          # years a branch shoot leafs out again from short shoots after its first; 0: none
param spur_share = 1;          # a short-shoot year's foliage spray, as a share of the shoot's first year's
param pipe_tip = 0.003;        # radius of the twig behind one living apex
param pipe_organ = 0.012;      # pipe radius per metre of spray bearing 0.25 m² a metre squared (revision 11)
param pipe_rings = 0.000016;   # ring area laid down per m² of foliage a year
param ring_share = 0;          # once the leader passes this share of height_max, each m² of foliage lays down less ring area, as (ring_share × height_max / height)^ring_power (taller trees grow less stem per leaf); 0: off
param ring_power = 2;
param adult_age = 20;          # the first year it is adult and bears cones
param cone_chance = 0.1;       # chance a year a well-lit branch apex bears one
param cone_light = 0.4;        # least light at an apex that bears
param cone_length = 0.08;
param cone_pitch = 100;        # degrees down from the shoot; negative stands it up
param cone_years = 2;          # years a cone stays on the tree
param cone_area = 0.55;        # area a cone 1 m long draws, m² (its template's)
param dioecious;               # 1: each plant is male or female; 0: every plant bears both
param sex;                     # this plant's own draw, 0 to 1: below a half, a dioecious plant is male
param wood_density = 800;      # kg/m³ of green wood
param wood_modulus = 8500;     # modulus of elasticity of green wood, MPa
param leaf_mass = 0.3;         # kg per m² of foliage spray
param bend_set = 0.5;          # share of each year's sag the wood keeps
param straighten = 0.02;       # share of its kept bend the wood straightens each year
param stem_bend = 0;           # share of its sag the stem takes: reaction wood holds it up
param reaction = 0.1;          # how far a year's ring turns a limb back up (bend@1)

module A queries light;                 # the leader's apex
module N;                               # nods a young leader shoot over
module C(order, vigour, starved, wood, held, years) queries light, position, substrate;  # a branch apex, wood metres from the stem, holding held metres grown over years years not yet laid down (seg_min)
module S queries vigour, substrate, position;  # the base of a living branch
module K;                               # the base of a dead branch
module W(n, m);                         # whorl branches still to be formed, of m
module I(n);                            # interwhorl branches still to be formed
module G(order, length, keep, wood, years);  # this year's shoot, grown over years years, its foliage kept keep years, wood metres from the stem
module L(order, wood, l);               # a possible side shoot on the left, after a shoot l long
module R(order, wood, l);               # and on the right
module Z(light);                        # a cone, perhaps, at a branch apex
module Y(n);                            # holds a shoot's foliage, which falls after n years
module V(order, wood, n, l);            # side shoots still to spread round a branch apex (revision 10)
module E(order, wood) queries light, position, substrate;  # a dormant bud where a branch shoot starts, watching the ground
module H queries substrate;             # where a branch shoot starts, its bud spent, watching the ground
module Q(n, s);                         # a branch shoot's short shoots: n more years to leaf out, a spray s long
module B(n);                            # n stems still to start

organ spray foliage area spray_area;
organ cone cone area cone_area;

tool light@1 { cell = 0.4, extinction = 0.35, bud = 0.01 };
tool vigour@1 { lambda = 0.7, alpha = 2 };
tool pipe@1 { exponent = pipe_exponent, tip = pipe_tip, organ = pipe_organ * pow(spray_area / 0.25, 1 / pipe_exponent), rings = pipe_rings * (ring_share > 0 ? min(1, pow(ring_share * height_max / max(0.1, richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape)), ring_power)) : 1) };
tool substrate@1 {};
tool bend@1 {
    density = wood_density,
    modulus = wood_modulus,
    leaf_mass = leaf_mass,
    set = bend_set,
    straighten = straighten,
    stem = stem_bend,
    ground = 0.02,
    reaction = reaction
};

axiom !(0.003) A B(stems - 1);


# The leader: this year's height growth with its foliage, then a whorl.
rule A -> G(0, (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape)
                - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))
               * (leader_shade + (1 - leader_shade) * light), needle_years * 2 / (1 + needle_low), 0, 1)
          W((bud_length > 0 ? round(whorl * min(1, (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape)
                - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))
               * (leader_shade + (1 - leader_shade) * light) / bud_length)) : whorl), (bud_length > 0 ? round(whorl * min(1, (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape)
                - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))
               * (leader_shade + (1 - leader_shade) * light) / bud_length)) : whorl)) /(137.5) A;

decompose W(n, m) : n > 0 ->
    [ /(360 * n / m + uniform(-20, 20)) &(branch_angle + uniform(-8, 8))
      T(0, 1, 0, upturn) S C(1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0) ]
    W(n - 1, m);
decompose W(n, m) -> ;
decompose I(n) : n > 0 ->
    [ /(uniform(0, 360)) &(branch_angle + uniform(-8, 8))
      T(0, 1, 0, upturn) S C(1, interwhorl_vigour, 0, 0, 0, 0) ]
    I(n - 1);
decompose I(n) -> ;

# A leader shoot nods over in two bends, which straighten over nod_years.
interpret N -> &(0.5 * nod * max(0, 1 - age / nod_years));

# An apex dies after shade_years starving in deep shade, once its shoot
# would be too short, on a side shoot after side_years if set, or when its
# drooping branch reaches the ground. Its shoot shortens with the wood
# between it and the stem (revision 5).
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) : sd < ground_gap + 0.05 -> ;
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) : light < shade_death && s + 1 >= shade_years -> ;
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) : o > 1 && side_years > 0 && age >= side_years -> ;
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) : o > side_order && twig_years > 0 && age >= twig_years -> ;
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) : v * pow(side_growth, o - 1) * (apical > 0 ? max(o > 1 ? max(branch_floor, side_floor * (floor_ramp > 0 ? min(1, (height - py) / floor_ramp) : 1)) : branch_floor, apical * (apical_light > 0 ? min(1, light / (apical_light * pow(min(1, shade_death / 0.1), 6))) : 1) * (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape) - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))) : branch_growth) / (1 + pow(w / branch_reach, reach_power))
                     * (0.3 + 0.7 * light) < min_shoot -> ;
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) : seg_min > 0 && height - py > top_ramp && a + v * pow(side_growth, o - 1) * (apical > 0 ? max(o > 1 ? max(branch_floor, side_floor * (floor_ramp > 0 ? min(1, (height - py) / floor_ramp) : 1)) : branch_floor, apical * (apical_light > 0 ? min(1, light / (apical_light * pow(min(1, shade_death / 0.1), 6))) : 1) * (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape) - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))) : branch_growth) / (1 + pow(w / branch_reach, reach_power)) * (top_ramp > 0 ? min(1, max(0.1, (height - py) / top_ramp)) : 1) * (0.3 + 0.7 * light) < seg_min ->
    C(o, v, light < shade_death ? s + 1 : 0, w + v * pow(side_growth, o - 1) * (apical > 0 ? max(o > 1 ? max(branch_floor, side_floor * (floor_ramp > 0 ? min(1, (height - py) / floor_ramp) : 1)) : branch_floor, apical * (apical_light > 0 ? min(1, light / (apical_light * pow(min(1, shade_death / 0.1), 6))) : 1) * (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape) - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))) : branch_growth) / (1 + pow(w / branch_reach, reach_power)) * (top_ramp > 0 ? min(1, max(0.1, (height - py) / top_ramp)) : 1) * (0.3 + 0.7 * light), a + v * pow(side_growth, o - 1) * (apical > 0 ? max(o > 1 ? max(branch_floor, side_floor * (floor_ramp > 0 ? min(1, (height - py) / floor_ramp) : 1)) : branch_floor, apical * (apical_light > 0 ? min(1, light / (apical_light * pow(min(1, shade_death / 0.1), 6))) : 1) * (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape) - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))) : branch_growth) / (1 + pow(w / branch_reach, reach_power)) * (top_ramp > 0 ? min(1, max(0.1, (height - py) / top_ramp)) : 1) * (0.3 + 0.7 * light), n + 1);
rule C(o, v, s, w, a, n) -> G(o, a + v * pow(side_growth, o - 1) * (apical > 0 ? max(o > 1 ? max(branch_floor, side_floor * (floor_ramp > 0 ? min(1, (height - py) / floor_ramp) : 1)) : branch_floor, apical * (apical_light > 0 ? min(1, light / (apical_light * pow(min(1, shade_death / 0.1), 6))) : 1) * (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape) - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))) : branch_growth) / (1 + pow(w / branch_reach, reach_power)) * (top_ramp > 0 ? min(1, max(0.1, (height - py) / top_ramp)) : 1) * (0.3 + 0.7 * light),
                        needle_years * 2 / (1 + needle_low) * (1 + (needle_low - 1) * max(0, 1 - py / max(height, 0.1))),
                        w - a, n + 1)
                      Z(light)
                      C(o, v, light < shade_death ? s + 1 : 0, w + v * pow(side_growth, o - 1) * (apical > 0 ? max(o > 1 ? max(branch_floor, side_floor * (floor_ramp > 0 ? min(1, (height - py) / floor_ramp) : 1)) : branch_floor, apical * (apical_light > 0 ? min(1, light / (apical_light * pow(min(1, shade_death / 0.1), 6))) : 1) * (richards(t + dt, height_max, height_rate, height_shape) - richards(t, height_max, height_rate, height_shape))) : branch_growth) / (1 + pow(w / branch_reach, reach_power)) * (top_ramp > 0 ? min(1, max(0.1, (height - py) / top_ramp)) : 1) * (0.3 + 0.7 * light), 0, 0);

# One year's leader shoot: its foliage, with the interwhorl branches halfway.
# A nodding leader (revision 12) carries its foliage in two sprays, one at
# the foot of each half, so the needles follow the bend: one spray from
# the foot ran on straight past it and left the nodding tip a bare stick.
# Each is 1/sqrt(2) of the one spray's size, so the leader's foliage area
# is unchanged.
decompose G(o, l, k, w, n) : o == 0 && nod > 0 ->
    N [ Y(k) spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.6 / sqrt(2)) ] F(l * 0.5) I(interwhorl)
    N [ Y(k) spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.6 / sqrt(2)) ] F(l * 0.5);
decompose G(o, l, k, w, n) : o == 0 ->
    [ Y(k) spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.6) ]
    N F(l * 0.5) I(interwhorl) N F(l * 0.5);
# One year's branch shoot: a spray along it and a twig spray to each side,
# hanging by spray_droop, each with its own turn; a dormant bud at its
# foot, which watches the ground; possible side shoots at its end. A shaded shoot is short, but its twigs
# and needles are not much smaller.
# With short shoots (revision 11) the shoot's sprays keep fixed turns, so
# the draws below keep their sites, and it leaves its short shoots (Q).
decompose G(o, l, k, w, n) : o > 0 && spur_years > 0 ->
    [ Y(k) spray(spray_base + spray_length * l, 0, spray_droop * 0.5, 1)
      spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.75, 90 + spray_droop * 0.6, twig_angle, 1)
      spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.75, -90 - spray_droop * 0.6, twig_angle, 1) ]
    E(o, w) Q(spur_years, ((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * spur_share) F(l) L(o, w + l, l) R(o, w + l, l);
# With twig_years, a twig (a shoot above side_order, which sets no side
# shoots and no dormant bud worth keeping) carries its year's foliage in one
# spray of the three's area (sqrt(1 + 2 * 0.75^2) = 1.458 times as long),
# and no bud: a third of the modules, so a fully branched crown fits the
# string to 300 years.
decompose G(o, l, k, w, n) : o > side_order && twig_years > 0 ->
    [ Y(k) spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 1.458, uniform(-25, 25), spray_droop * 0.5, 1) ]
    F(l);
decompose G(o, l, k, w, n) ->
    [ Y(k) spray(spray_base + spray_length * l, uniform(-25, 25), spray_droop * 0.5, 1)
      spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.75, 90 + spray_droop * 0.6 + uniform(-20, 20), twig_angle, 1)
      spray(((spray_base + spray_length * l / n) * sqrt(n)) * 0.75, -90 - spray_droop * 0.6 + uniform(-20, 20), twig_angle, 1) ]
    E(o, w) F(l) L(o, w + l, l) R(o, w + l, l);
decompose L(o, w, l) : apex_whorl <= 0 && o <= side_order && rand() < side_chance * (bud_length > 0 ? min(1, l / bud_length) : 1) -> [ +(side_angle) S C(o + 1, 1, 0, w, 0, 0) ];
decompose L(o, w, l) : apex_whorl <= 0 -> ;
decompose R(o, w, l) : apex_whorl <= 0 && o <= side_order && rand() < side_chance * (bud_length > 0 ? min(1, l / bud_length) : 1) -> [ -(side_angle) S C(o + 1, 1, 0, w, 0, 0) ];
decompose R(o, w, l) : apex_whorl <= 0 -> ;

# Foliage falls after its shoot's keep years: needle_years on average,
# needle_low times as long at the crown's foot as at its top (revision 5).
rule Y(n) : age + dt >= n -> %;

# A well-lit apex of a tree old enough bears a cone, which falls after
# cone_years; on a dioecious species, only a female tree does.
decompose Z(l) : t >= adult_age && l >= cone_light && rand() < cone_chance
                 && (dioecious < 0.5 || sex >= 0.5) ->
    [ $ /(uniform(-20, 20)) &(cone_pitch + uniform(-15, 15)) cone(cone_length * uniform(0.85, 1.15)) ];
decompose Z(l) -> ;
rule cone(s) : age + dt >= cone_years -> ;

# A branch with no living apex dies, and falls dead_years later.
rule S : sd < ground_gap -> %;
rule S : age > 2 && ntip == 0 -> K;
rule S : crown_ratio > 0 && age > 2 && py < (1 - crown_ratio) * height -> K;
rule S : bole > 0 && age > 2 && py < bole * pow(min(1, height / height_max), bole_shape) * height -> K;
rule K : age >= dead_years -> %;

# Revision 5: a branch that sags to within ground_gap of the ground (or
# of the substrate) is shed from there out.
rule H : sd < ground_gap -> %;

# Revision 5: a branch shoot's dormant bud sprouts a side shoot after
# epi_delay years where light reaches it (epicormic shoots). Once it has
# sprouted or waited epi_years it is spent (H) and only watches the
# ground. A bud beyond the orders that branch is spent at once, and
# goes unless it is within ground_watch of the ground; the bud at a
# branch's foot, on the stem, never sprouts.
# A spent bud more than 2 m above ground_watch goes too (revision 13):
# kept on every limb, spent buds filled the module string of old trees.
rule E(o, w) : o > (epi_order > 0 ? epi_order : side_order) && py > ground_watch -> ;
rule E(o, w) : sd < ground_gap -> %;
rule E(o, w) : (epi_delay <= 0 || age >= epi_years || o > (epi_order > 0 ? epi_order : side_order)) && py > ground_watch + 2 -> ;
rule E(o, w) : epi_delay <= 0 || age >= epi_years || o > (epi_order > 0 ? epi_order : side_order) -> H;
rule E(o, w) : w > 0 && age >= epi_delay && light >= epi_light && rand() < epi_chance ->
    [ /(uniform(0, 360)) &(side_angle) S C(o + 1, 1, 0, w, 0, 0) ] H;

# Revision 10: with apex_whorl, a branch apex spreads its side shoots
# round itself, as the trunk spreads a whorl, each turning up toward the
# sky (Rauh's model). These rules go last so the draws above keep their
# sites.
decompose L(o, w, l) : o <= side_order -> V(o, w, apex_whorl, l);
decompose L(o, w, l) -> ;
decompose R(o, w, l) -> ;
decompose V(o, w, n, l) : n > 0 && rand() < side_chance * (bud_length > 0 ? min(1, l / bud_length) : 1) ->
    [ /(360 * n / max(apex_whorl, 1) + uniform(-30, 30)) &(side_angle + uniform(-8, 8))
      T(0, 1, 0, upturn) S C(o + 1, 1, 0, w, 0, 0) ]
    V(o, w, n - 1, l);
decompose V(o, w, n, l) : n > 0 -> V(o, w, n - 1, l);
decompose V(o, w, n, l) -> ;

# Short shoots (revision 11): a branch shoot leafs out again each year
# from its short shoots, spur_years after its first, along its own line.
# These rules go last so the draws above keep their sites.
decompose Q(n, s) : n <= 0 -> ;
rule Q(n, s) : n > 0 ->
    [ Y(needle_years) spray(s, uniform(-25, 25), spray_droop * 0.5, 1) ] Q(n - 1, s);

# Several stems (revision 12; these rules go last, so the draws above keep
# their sites): a clump grows stems - 1 more leaders from the first one's
# foot, each leaning stem_angle out, spread evenly round; one stem grows as
# before.
decompose B(n) : n > 0 ->
    [ /(360 * n / stems + uniform(-25, 25)) &(stem_angle + uniform(-5, 5)) A ] B(n - 1);
decompose B(n) -> ;

# Revision 13: a limb older than limb_age whose tips catch too little light
# for the wood they carry is shed (Palubicki et al. 2009, as broadleaf's
# shed_ratio, but named apart: the self-pruning rule's broadleaf value,
# 0.05, bound here too and starved ponderosa pine by 60 years). The share
# scales with shade_death, so a shade-tolerant
# crown keeps its dim limbs longer. This rule goes last so the draws above
# keep their sites.
rule S : limb_light > 0 && age > limb_age && qsum < limb_light * shade_death / 0.1 * nseg -> K;
rule S : limb_tip > 0 && age > limb_age && qsum < limb_tip * shade_death * ntip -> K;
